    "status": "SUCCESS",
    "response": "It is understandable to feel frustrated when a hiring process concludes without the outcome you hoped for, especially given your formal leadership credentials and administrative experience. \n\nHowever, looking at the situation objectively:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Is an all-female panel a sign of bias on its own?\n**In isolation, no.** \n* **Role-based composition:** Interview panels are typically composed based on institutional roles (e.g., district superintendent, head of HR, a fellow principal, school board/union representative) rather than gender balancing. If the people holding those offices are women, the panel will naturally reflect that.\n* **Legal standard:** In employment law and public-sector appointments, the mere demographic makeup of an interview panel does not constitute evidence of bias or unlawful discrimination. \n\n---\n\n### 2. Qualifications on Paper vs. Selection Criteria\nWhile your CV demonstrates direct administrative experience (5 years as deputy principal and a master’s in educational leadership), headship appointments rarely hinge solely on paper credentials:\n* **Interview performance & vision:** Final-round interviews usually focus on institutional vision, change management, crisis response, and school-specific strategic priorities.\n* **School fit & references:** Panels often weigh candidate references, pedagogical leadership style, and specific community or faculty needs that may favor a candidate with deep classroom experience (10 years) and targeted leadership coursework.\n* **Standardized scoring:** Most formal recruitment processes use a structured rubric. A candidate with less administrative title experience can outperform on specific situational questions or strategic exercises.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Should you make an issue of it?\n**Not unless you have concrete, documented evidence of procedural irregularity or discriminatory conduct.**\n\n* **Risks of an unsubstantiated claim:** Suggesting gender bias solely because the panel was female and the selected candidate was female carries significant professional risk. It can damage your reputation within the school district or regional education community.\n* **What constitutes actual evidence:** Tangible grounds for concern would involve things such as:\n  * Inappropriate, gender-targeted questions during interviews.\n  * Clear deviations from mandatory hiring policies or scoring rubrics.\n  * Disregard of mandatory qualification thresholds defined in the original job advertisement.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Recommended Next Steps\n\n1. **Follow up on your feedback request politely:**  \n   Two weeks is common for HR departments, especially if the final contract with the appointed principal is still being finalized. Send a brief, professional follow-up:\n   > *\"I am following up on my request of [Date] regarding feedback on the selection process. As I continue to develop my leadership career, detailed constructive feedback on my application and interview performance would be very valuable.\"*\n\n2. **Offer a phone/in-person option:**  \n   Panels are often hesitant to commit extensive feedback to writing for fear of litigation. Offering a brief 10–15 minute phone call with the panel chair often yields more candid, actionable insight into where your candidacy fell short.\n\n3. **Check local appointment regulations:**  \n   If this is a public school or civil service appointment, review the formal rules in your jurisdiction:\n   * Many jurisdictions have formal transparency or freedom-of-information rules regarding candidate scoring sheets or summary qualification assessments.\n   * Check whether strict deadlines exist if you intend to exercise any formal right of appeal or access to records.\n",
